// One test's outstanding work is that test's, and `schedule` is the only thing that can say so.
//
// **The second consumer `schedule` has ever had.** `TECHNICAL.md` argues it at length and
// `vision/std/platform.wac`'s `Sys.drain` is the only place that uses it — one use, so nothing has
// tested whether the primitive generalises. This is a second, and it is a real problem rather than
// an exercise.
//
// ## The problem, in the shipped tree
//
// `std/platform.wac` says of `drain`: *"A `main` that schedules and returns without draining leaves
// work outstanding, which the host treats as an error; `dropAll` is how a program says it meant
// to."* The host asks `outstanding()` **before deciding a clean exit** — once, at the end of the
// program.
//
// A test runner runs many tests in one program against one `Core`, so there is one queue for all of
// them. A test that schedules a continuation and returns without draining does three things, none
// of which anything reports:
//
//   - its work runs during **some later test**, whichever one next dispatches;
//   - that later test's failure is attributed to the later test;
//   - and if nothing dispatches, the whole program is marked unclean at the end, naming nobody.
//
// The middle one is the expensive one. A flake that only appears when two particular tests run in
// one process, and moves when the order changes, is the hardest failure this repository has.
//
// ## What `schedule` does about it
//
// `schedule <target>` redirects where continuations land for the rest of the enclosing scope, and
// the operator's own wrinkle about it is the part that makes this work: **the target is restored
// because the scope resumes**, not because anybody resets it. So a runner can give each test its own
// queue by writing one statement inside the loop body, and the restoration is the block ending.

import { Result, Ticket, Queue, Continuation } from "core";
import { T, Report } from "./assert.wac";

/** What a test left behind. */
export struct Leaked { string test; i32 count; }

/**
 * Run `body`, and answer what it scheduled and did not drain.
 *
 * The whole of the isolation is the `schedule` on the third line. Everything a test schedules —
 * directly, or inside anything it calls, including a nested `Tasks.drain` — lands in `mine` instead
 * of on the caller's queue, until this block ends.
 *
 * **It took a `Sys` and never used it**, found while deleting the bundle. Nothing in the body ever
 * mentioned the parameter: the isolation is `schedule`, which needs no capability, and the queue it
 * redirects to is a local. So this function held authority over a filesystem, a network and a
 * process table in order to run a lambda.
 *
 * That is `packages/box`'s measurement — *ten applets of sixty-three never mention `fs`* — happening
 * in `vision/` itself, one file away from the paragraph arguing against it, and it took until the
 * bundle was removed for anyone to look. A bundle makes an unused capability free to accept and
 * invisible afterwards; a parameter list makes it a word somebody has to type and a reader can see.
 */
export async Result<Report, Leaked> isolated(string name, fn<Ticket<Report>()> body) {
  Queue<Continuation> mine = Queue.create();
  schedule mine.push;

  Report r = await body();

  // Everything the test meant to happen has to have happened by now. Draining here rather than
  // refusing outright is the choice worth arguing: a test that schedules and expects the runner to
  // dispatch is writing against the shipped behaviour, and refusing it would be a new rule rather
  // than a new report. So it runs, and the count is reported.
  i32 ran = 0;
  while (mine.len() > 0) {
    Continuation c = mine.pop()!;
    await c.t;
    c.call();
    ran = ran + 1;
  }
  return ran > 0 ? Err(Leaked(name, ran)) : Ok(r);
}
